Here's my analysis of `InputValidatorTests.cs`:

---

## Test Maintainability Report

### Summary
**1 major finding** — Category 3 (copy-paste test methods). Your 9 tests are structurally identical and are a textbook candidate for parameterized tests via `[DataRow]`.

---

### Finding: Copy-Paste Methods → `[DataRow]`

Every test follows the exact same pattern:
```csharp
var validator = new InputValidator();
var result = validator.Validate(<input>);
Assert.Is{True|False}(result.IsValid);
Assert.AreEqual(<expectedError>, result.ErrorMessage);
```

**Suggested refactoring:**

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class InputValidatorTests
{
    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("",        false, "Input cannot be empty",               DisplayName = "Empty string")]
    [DataRow("ab",      false, "Input must be at least 3 characters", DisplayName = "Too short")]
    [DataRow("hello<script>", false, "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
    [DataRow(null,      false, "Input cannot be empty",               DisplayName = "Null")]
    [DataRow("   ",    false, "Input cannot be empty",               DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
    [DataRow("hello-world", true, null,                              DisplayName = "Valid input")]
    [DataRow("abc",    true, null,                                   DisplayName = "Min boundary")]
    public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string? input, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow(256, false, "Input must not exceed 255 characters", DisplayName = "Too long")]
    [DataRow(255, true,  null,                                   DisplayName = "Max boundary")]
    public void Validate_LengthBoundary_ReturnsExpected(int length, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(new string('a', length));
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }
}
```

---

### Impact
| Metric | Before | After |
|--------|--------|-------|
| Methods | 9 | 2 |
| Lines | ~80 | ~30 |
| Adding a case | New method (~7 lines) | One `[DataRow]` line |

### Trade-offs
- ✅ Adding a new test case is now a single `[DataRow]` attribute — no new method needed.
- ✅ Test Explorer still shows each case individually thanks to `DisplayName`.
- ⚠️ Slight loss of per-test self-documentation (mitigated by clear `DisplayName` values).
- ⚠️ The length-boundary tests need a separate method since `new string(...)` isn't a compile-time constant — this is the correct split.

Would you like me to apply this refactoring to your file?
